---

27. TYPOGRAPHY

Use an excellent readable Latin typeface.

Support Devanāgarī/Sanskrit properly when terms or source passages require it.

But the primary interface should remain readable to someone who knows no Sanskrit.

Show:

Ghaṭikā
घटिका

only where useful.

Do not fill the app with decorative Devanāgarī.

Transliteration should be technically correct.

---

28. CONTENT STYLE

Scientific but accessible.

Bad:

"Ancient Indians had an incredibly advanced understanding of astronomy."

Good:

"By combining measured time, angular motion and eclipse geometry, siddhānta astronomers could calculate when the Sun, Moon and lunar node approached an eclipse configuration."

Never tell users that something is remarkable.

Let the interactive result demonstrate it.

Avoid civilizational boasting.

Avoid civilizational dismissal.

Treat the material as history of quantitative science.

---

29. TECH STACK

Preferred unless repo constraints dictate otherwise:

Next.js
TypeScript
React
Tailwind CSS
SVG
Framer Motion only where useful
D3 only if genuinely needed for plotting/scales
Astronomy Engine or independently validated astronomical package for modern reference

Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

No Three.js unless a genuinely 3D celestial-sphere view proves substantially better than SVG/canvas.

I prefer the application to remain lightweight.

---

30. NUMERICAL ARCHITECTURE

Separate physics/history from presentation.

For example:

"src/lib/time/"
traditional time conversion

"src/lib/solar/"
gnomon and solar geometry

"src/lib/water-clock/"
water-clock reconstruction

"src/lib/astronomy/"
modern astronomy

"src/lib/historical-astronomy/"
historical models

"src/lib/uncertainty/"
error propagation / Monte Carlo

"src/components/instruments/"
SVG instruments

"src/components/plots/"

"src/content/"

"src/data/sources/"

Do not bury mathematical calculations inside React components.

All quantitative functions need unit tests.

---

31. TESTS

Create tests for:

Time conversion:
24 modern hours = 60 ghaṭikā under the chosen convention.

Gnomon:
known solar-altitude geometry.

East-west construction:
synthetic test cases.

Tithi:
known Sun/Moon elongations.

Historical astronomy:
published worked examples where available.

Modern astronomy:
known eclipse cases.

Historical-vs-modern:
regression cases.

Water-clock:
conservation and monotonicity assumptions.

Do not tune historical constants merely to make the historical answers agree with modern results.

That would invalidate the experiment.

---

32. VALIDATION DATASET

Build:

"data/eclipse-benchmarks.json"

Prefer eclipses for which reliable historical/scholarly information exists.

Fields:

date
location
eclipse type
historical source/model
historical predicted contacts
historical observation if available
modern reconstructed contacts
ΔT assumption
uncertainty
citation

Start small but defensible.

Five strong benchmark events are better than fifty poorly sourced ones.

Later include observational work by astronomers such as Parameśvara where academically appropriate, clearly identifying this as medieval rather than ancient.

